okay today I'm going to talk to you  about using a light microscope here is  an image of a light microscope what we  use microscopes for is to be able to  view tiny things it magnifies them so we  can see them in much more detail or even  see things that we wouldn't otherwise be  able to see with the naked eye they're  different from telescopes because  telescopes are used to view really  distant things such as planets or stars  microscopes are used to look at things  that are microscopic very small so um  before we get onto actually using the  microscope if we want to view something  with a microscope first of all we have  to prepare it by putting it onto a slide  now all a slide is is it's a bit of  glass that our sample sits on glasses  it's important that it's glass because  it needs to be transparent so let's say  I have some thin slice of onion really  thin slice of onion and I've put it on  my slide now what I might need I might  need four different it depends on what  I'm looking at but I might need  something called a stain so all a stain  is is a liquid that you can you can drop  on to your sample and it kind of stains  different parts of the cell for example  if you have a set stain called methyl  blue it stains the nuclei so that's all  nuclei remember is just a plural of  nucleus it stains all the nuclei so you  can see them much better  so after you've put the stain on there  you put on this little square of glass  called a coverslip just to keep  everything in place so the coverslip  goes out over the top there and you just  need to press it down to get rid of any  air bubbles as well so then this is  ready your specimen is now ready to be  viewed on the microscope so it's on that  slide so it sits it goes on here now  this particular part here where your  specimen goes you can actually clip it  down with these clips this part here is  called the stage so this is where your  specimen actually sits right here now  it's really important as well that we  turn the microscope on because this part  here at the bottom this part here is the  light so it's important that we have  light shining up through the sample like  this so we can actually be the sample  itself okay  the specimen right the other things that  we need to know the bit that you look  down this part here is called the  eyepiece lens nicer simply it goes with  your eye so this here is the eyepiece  lens that you look down the name of the  other lens is here of the objective lens  objective lenses can you see there's  several different objective lenses all  of them have different strengths of  magnification  some of them have a lower magnification  some have a higher magnification and  also this part here this is called the  focusing wheel so you turn this in order  to bring the image into focus now what  you need to do to begin with once you've  set up your microscope you've got your  specimen ready it's on the stage you  start off with the stage low so you have  it as low as it goes to begin with then  what you do is you choose the objective  lens with the lowest magnification the  weakest magnification out of all of  these what you then do is when that's  all set up and remember your light is on  whilst looking down the eyepiece lens  you turn the focusing wheel bringing up  the stage until it comes into focus so  that means you could see it clearly it's  no longer blurry what you can then do is  a lot of microscopes actually have a  fine focusing wheel as well so the  bigger one here this is the kind of  course focusing to get it pretty much  roughly in focus and this smaller one  here is to get it perfectly focused now  once you've got the image in focus you  can then change the magnification by  changing the objective lens so this part  here just swivels around  and you could pick whichever lens you  want to pick after you've swiveled round  and chose the lens it might need some  more fine focusing in order to get it  and focus but that's essentially it that  is how to use a light microscope  so just to recap you need to put your  specimen on a slide you might need to  stain it so we can see it better under  the microscope and you put on a little  thing called a coverslip it's a bit of  glass that goes over the top that  specimen goes onto the stage which is  this part here of the microscope we  always start with a stage down then  whilst looking through the eyepiece lens  we slowly slowly bring the stage up  until it's into focus in focus sorry  then we always start with a weakest  magnification objective lens want it in  focus that's when we could change the  strength of the objective lens and  that's how to use a light microscope  thank you
 

